============================================================================
5. INDIGENOUS DATA SOVEREIGNTY AND AI RESTRICTIONS
============================================================================

5.1  NO AI TRAINING — condition of license: no training, fine-tuning,
     aligning, evaluating, augmenting, or distilling Models on the Work,
     Derivative Data, or outputs; no inclusion in corpora, RAG indices, or
     embedding stores (Jacobsen condition doctrine).

5.2  TDM RIGHTS RESERVED, including under Directive (EU) 2019/790 Art. 4(3);
     machine-readable reservations accompany the Work.

5.3  DERIVATIVE MODELS/DATA are asserted to be infringing to the maximum
     extent of law and remain subject to this License (cf. Thomson Reuters
     v. Ross Intelligence, No. 20-cv-613 (D. Del. Feb. 11, 2025); generative-
     AI fair use unsettled as of 2026 — this condition operates regardless).

5.4  PATENT/DESIGN DISCLOSURE CONDITION adopting WIPO GRATK Treaty Art. 3's
     disclosure principle as contract, notwithstanding US non-party status.

5.5  NO SURVEILLANCE, biometric exploitation, or identity profiling of
     Community members.

5.6  DELETION + ATTESTATION on termination: delete Work and Derivative Data
     (backups within 90 days), quarantine violating Models, officer-signed
     attestation within 14 days.

============================================================================
6. RESERVED SOVEREIGN RIGHTS AND TREATY FOUNDATIONS  [ADAPT]
============================================================================

6.1  RESERVED RIGHTS. The Nation reserved all rights not expressly ceded in
     <|specific_treaty_1_name_year_stat_cite|> and
     <|specific_treaty_2_name_year_stat_cite|> (United States v. Winans,
     198 U.S. 371 (1905); Winters, 207 U.S. 564 (1908); Minnesota v. Mille
     Lacs Band, 526 U.S. 172 (1999)). Treaty rights are federal law paramount
     over state law (U.S. Const. art. VI, cl. 2; McGirt v. Oklahoma, 140
     S. Ct. 2452 (2020)).

6.2  HONEST ARCHITECTURE. The Supremacy Clause governs treaty-versus-state
     conflicts; a private license is not "superior to other contracts." This
     License's force comes from: copyright condition doctrine (2.2); the
     Licensee's consent (Montana, 450 U.S. at 565); federal cultural-property
     statutes (NAGPRA; IACA); and the treaty-protected status of the
     underlying rights defended here.

6.3  INDIAN CANONS: ambiguities favor the tribal interest where a tribunal
     so permits (Blackfeet, 471 U.S. 759 (1985); Bryan, 426 U.S. 373 (1976);
     Oneida, 470 U.S. 226 (1985)).

6.4  LIVING JURISPRUDENCE. [ADAPT — cite your Nation's modern
     acknowledgments, e.g., consent decrees, settlements, or 21st-century
     rulings on your treaties.]

============================================================================
7. TREATY WATERS AND MARITIME DIMENSION  [OPTIONAL — coastal/Great Lakes/
riverine Nations only; otherwise DELETE and renumber]
============================================================================

7.1  TREATY WATERS. Knowledge of waterways, fisheries, navigation, and
     place-names within ceded waters is Treaty-adjacent TK; the Work shall
     not be used to materially harm treaty-managed fisheries, habitats, or
     co-management regimes.

7.2  RESERVED SUBMERGED LANDS AND WATER RIGHTS preserved: tribal bed
     ownership where reserved (Montana, 450 U.S. 544 (1981); Choctaw Nation
     v. Oklahoma, 397 U.S. 620 (1970); Holt State Bank, 270 U.S. 49 (1926));
     Winters reserved water rights (207 U.S. 564 (1908)).

7.3  ADMIRALTY PRESERVATION. 28 U.S.C. § 1333 jurisdiction is neither
     created nor waived; Licensees are barred from maritime uses of the Work
     that materially interfere with Treaty-Protected Interests (e.g.,
     treaty fishing); remedies in admiralty, federal question, and tribal
     forums preserved without election.

7.4  CUSTOMARY INTERNATIONAL LAW. CIL is part of US law (The Paquete
     Habana, 175 U.S. 677, 700 (1900)). UNCLOS is cited honestly: US
     non-party; navigational/resource norms accepted as CIL (US Oceans
     Policy Statement, 1983).

7.5  MARINE TREATY-RIGHTS LINEAGE (e.g., Treaty of Neah Bay (1855),
     12 Stat. 939; Makah litigation, Anderson v. Evans (9th Cir. 2004);
     Boldt-decision line) recorded as precedent for recognition of reserved
     marine usufructuary rights.

7.6  BOUNDARY WATERS (where applicable): Boundary Waters Treaty of 1909
     (36 Stat. 2448) interests preserved; no use of the Work to undermine
     Indigenous navigation, fishing, or stewardship in boundary waters.

============================================================================
8. CULTURAL PATRIMONY, BURIAL PROTECTIONS, AND ANTI-ALIENATION
============================================================================

8.1  NON-ALIENATION. No use of the Work to facilitate sale, transfer,
     marketing, appraisal for conveyance, or other alienation of human
     remains, funerary objects, sacred objects, burial sites, or objects of
     Cultural Patrimony (NAGPRA, 25 U.S.C. §§ 3001-3002).

8.2  SEPULCHER AND NAGPRA PRINCIPLES as conditions: no disturbing or
     commodifying resting places of ancestors (25 U.S.C. §§ 3001-3013).

8.3  DISCOVERY PROTOCOL: burial/site information encountered through the
     Work is restricted Derivative Data — no publication, no commercial use,
     prompt notice, referral to tribal authorities.

8.4  IACA PRESERVATION. For material offered in commerce as authentic
     Indigenous production: civil recovery of treble damages or not less
     than $1,000 per day of violation (25 U.S.C. § 305e(b)); federal crimes
     (18 U.S.C. §§ 1158, 1159) remain federal. Statutory remedies supplement
     this License.

