11. PERMISSIBLE USES AND COMMUNITY BENEFIT
============================================================================

11.1 PERMISSIBLE USES: education with attribution and context; research with
     findings shared with the Licensor; non-commercial Community development
     with notice; uses promoting accurate cultural understanding; culturally
     appropriate engagement.

11.2 COMMUNITY BENEFIT LICENSEES. Community members (Section 1.8) receive a
     standing no-cost license for non-commercial cultural, educational,
     language-revitalization, stewardship, and community-development uses,
     conditioned on Sections 3, 8, and Community Protocols — delivering the
     Collective Benefit required by the CARE Principles.

============================================================================
12. SOVEREIGN REVOCATION, TERMINATION, AND COMPLIANCE
============================================================================

12.1 REVOCATION: (a) automatic, immediate termination for material breach;
     (b) as to TK/TCE-bearing components, revocation on 30 days' notice for
     reasons of cultural integrity, Community Protocols, or defense of
     Treaty-Protected Interests.

12.2 FALLBACK LADDER (revocation): if (b) fails — (i) breach-termination
     stands; (ii) 90-day protocol-cause revocation; (iii) fixed-term grant
     with surviving conditions; (iv) § 502 injunction for out-of-scope use.

12.3 COMPLIANCE DUTIES on revocation: cease use; delete copies and
     Derivative Data; quarantine violating Models; officer-signed attestation
     in 14 days; deliver-up/destruction of infringing articles consistent
     with 17 U.S.C. § 503.

12.4 CIVIL ONLY. This License creates no criminal sanctions; criminal
     statutes cited are federal, enforced by federal authorities; tribal
     criminal jurisdiction over nonmembers is not asserted (Oliphant, 435
     U.S. 191 (1978)).

============================================================================
13. COMPENSATION, DAMAGES, AND REMEDIES
============================================================================

13.1 ELECTION: actual damages + profits (§ 504(b)); or statutory damages
     (§ 504(c), up to $150,000 per work for willful infringement — the
     congressional benchmark); plus fees (§ 505); CMI remedies (§ 1203).

13.2 CONTRACTUAL DAMAGES — PRIMARY ASSERTION: royalty ≥ 20% of gross revenue
     from unauthorized use; liquidated damages of $10,000/day after notice;
     disgorgement of benefit at fair market value — stipulated as a
     reasonable pre-estimate of difficult-to-quantify harm, including
     cultural and spiritual harm.

13.3 FALLBACK LADDER (damages): judicially reduced liquidated damages →
     § 504(c) statutory (willful max) → IACA $1,000/day floor where
     Indigenous-authenticity misrepresentation applies (25 U.S.C.
     § 305e(b)) → actual + § 504(b) profits → restitution. Failure of any
     rung does not affect the rungs below.

13.4 ADDITIONAL RECOVERABLES: legal fees, cultural restoration costs, public
     correction costs, compliance-verification costs.

13.5 COURT-ORDERED DISPOSITION ONLY (17 U.S.C. § 503) — no self-help
     seizure of equipment, domains, or accounts.

13.6 LIMITATIONS HONESTY: § 507(b) three-year period governs copyright
     claims, with continuing violations accruing daily; revocation of
     cultural-protocol conditions is continuing and not waived by delay;
     advance waivers of limitations defenses are excised under Section 17.

============================================================================
14. DISPUTE RESOLUTION: TRIBAL COURTS, EXHAUSTION, COMITY, ARBITRATION
============================================================================

14.1 CONSENT JURISDICTION. Acceptance forms a Consensual Relationship
     (Montana, 450 U.S. at 565). Every Licensee consents to the civil
     jurisdiction of the <|tribal_court_name|> for License disputes and
     waives personal-jurisdiction objections for such disputes.

14.2 PRIMARY FORUM; EXHAUSTION. <|tribal_court_name|> is primary for
     cultural/protocol conditions; tribal exhaustion applies to tribal-
     jurisdiction questions (National Farmers Union, 471 U.S. 845 (1985);
     Iowa Mutual, 480 U.S. 9 (1987)). Copyright claims are exclusive to
     federal court (28 U.S.C. § 1338); sequencing at the Licensor's election,
     tribal rulings first where the claims interlock.

14.3 ALTERNATIVE TRIBAL FORUM. If the primary forum declines or lacks
     jurisdiction, the Licensor may designate another federally recognized
     tribal court or traditional forum willing to accept the matter,
     preferably connected to the Nation; the Licensee's consent extends.

14.4 RECOGNITION — CORRECT MECHANISM. § 1738 does not govern tribal
     judgments; federal recognition rests on comity (Wilson v. Marchington,
     127 F.3d 805 (9th Cir. 1997)); the Licensee stipulates the designated
     forums afford impartial tribunals and due process; state-recognition
     rules (e.g., Michigan Court Rule 2.615) apply where relevant; statutory
     full-faith-and-credit exceptions (25 U.S.C. § 1911(d); 28 U.S.C.
     § 1738B; 18 U.S.C. § 2265) are unaffected.

14.5 WAIVER of forum non conveniens and similar discretionary objections, to
     the extent enforceable.

14.6 FAA ARBITRATION TRACK (Licensor's election): Indian-law-expert
     arbitrators; award confirmable under 9 U.S.C. § 9 (structure informed by
     C&L Enterprises, 532 U.S. 411 (2001)).

14.7 FEDERAL ACCESS PRESERVED (28 U.S.C. §§ 1331, 1338, 1362) plus interim
     injunctive relief anywhere competent.

